Signed-off-by: Kees Cook <keescook@chromium.org>
Reviewed-by: Gustavo A. R. Silva <gustavoars@kernel.org>
Acked-by: Serge Semin <fancer.lancer@gmail.com>
Link: https://lore.kernel.org/r/20210211203714.1929862-1-keescook@chromium.org
Signed-off-by: Mark Brown <broonie@kernel.org>

Here is the root cause:
The bug was caused by incorrect pointer arithmetic when copying memory. The original code used a pointer to `u32` for the `data` variable, which resulted in the `memcpy` function interpreting the offset in `u32` units instead of bytes. This led to potential out-of-bounds access when the offset exceeded the size of the `data` variable, exposing stack content and causing undefined behavior.

Here is the patch:
  */
 static void dw_spi_bt1_dirmap_copy_from_map(void *to, void __iomem *from, size_t len)
 {
 	size_t shift, chunk;
 	u32 data;

 	/*
 	 * We split the copying up into the next three stages: unaligned head,
 	 * aligned body, unaligned tail.
 	 */
 	shift = (size_t)from & 0x3;
 	if (shift) {
 		chunk = min_t(size_t, 4 - shift, len);
 		data = readl_relaxed(from - shift);
-		memcpy(to, &data + shift, chunk);
+		memcpy(to, (char *)&data + shift, chunk);
 		from += chunk;
 		to += chunk;
 		len -= chunk;
 	}

 	while (len >= 4) {
 		data = readl_relaxed(from);
 		memcpy(to, &data, 4);
 		from += 4;
 		to += 4;
 		len -= 4;
 	}

 	if (len) {
 		data = readl_relaxed(from);
 		memcpy(to, &data, len);
 	}
 }



